# I Alone Am the Leader

*(Not a poem; it may be made into one)*

Henry VIII once ruled fair England. With the Pope's consent he married Catherine, his brother's widow. While they lived together, Protestant reformers rose against corruption in the Church and the Pope's authority. Henry then defended the old faith so ardently, even writing a book against the reformers, that he was honoured as a protector of religion.

But Catherine grew older and had borne him no son. More importantly, the lovely Anne Boleyn, one of the queen's attendants, had captivated him. Henry now declared that marrying his brother's widow had been sinful and begged the Pope to annul the marriage. The Pope delayed: Catherine was a Spanish princess, and Spain was then a mighty power. When permission was finally refused, Henry's devotion changed into fury.

He suddenly discovered endless corruption in papal rule. Why, he asked, should England continue to seek Rome's permission for everything? Let a new order prevail; let Protestantism enter; let Rome be cast aside. The law followed the king's command. Catherine departed and Anne Boleyn arrived. The hidden truth was simple: the king made himself the authority able to grant the permission that Rome denied him. A problem of the royal bed transformed the country's religious order.

Many nevertheless praised Henry's courage and credited him with bringing reform to England. Yet his motive was not religion but desire for a woman. Is it not likewise possible that some who change their principles and appearances have reasons other than principle? When people fail to obtain what they seek, they often begin finding fault with everything they formerly praised.

If Henry were asked why the Pope whom he once revered had suddenly become unacceptable, he would say, "I spoke then in ignorance; today I have discovered a rare truth." Who would dare ask whether that truth was named Anne Boleyn? The questioner's head might roll. He was king before and king afterward; who could contradict him?

Therefore, dear comrades, when someone who once proclaimed the joy of Dravida suddenly calls it a mirage and a useless tale, ask for the reason. But an autocrat need offer none. One day he may call Dravida bliss and deny it the next, confident that a crowd will follow both commands. Without the slightest shame he will cry, "I alone am the leader! I alone am the leader!" Those who walk the path such men show become slaves and find only suffering. The world holds a thousand proofs. Know this, and act with discernment.

*(4 June 1961)*

*(A satirical portrait drawn when E. V. K. Sampath left the DMK, exposing his lack of consistent principle.)*
